From b6ceeeb868c7f92d666c65aa669c6e331514195c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MiaAI-Lab <MiaAI-Lab@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:24:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 192/194] glm_moe_dsa: exact decode indexer candidates, policy
 bits 2 and 3 (RoPE hoist, multi-CTA top-2048)

Two exact candidates for a decode window's DSA select (sparse.dsaSelect, at most 16
rows, no prompt split). Both are off by default. Each one turns on from a request's
policy bit (TF_GLM_POLICY_REQUESTS=1, night/swift/POLICY.md) or for every forward from
its boot switch.

- Bit 2, rope_hoist (TF_GLM_DSA_ROPE_HOIST=1): tf_glm_dsa_qrope ropes the index queries
  once a row into private scratch (Scratch.qr, a decode buffer's [rows][32][128] bf16).
  It writes the bf16 values tf_glm_dsa_scores' in-place rope leaves in shared memory.
  qi itself stays unroped. tf_glm_dsa_scores_roped is that kernel line for line, but
  without the rope block and its two barriers. A CTA then scores 1, 4 or 8 key blocks
  (more rows, more blocks). The scale, ReLU, MMA and head reduction are unchanged.
  Taken from 2 rows past sincosf's fast path, 3-4 rows from 12,288 keys, and 5+ rows
  from 8,192 keys.
- Bit 3, topk_multi_cta (TF_GLM_DSA_TOPK_MULTI=1): tf_glm_dsa_tscan and tf_glm_dsa_tmerge
  (glm_dsa_topk_multi.cuh) replace the single 1,024-thread tf_glm_dsa_topk CTA a row
  from 24,576 keys. Every scan CTA computes the same sampled floor and writes gt/eq bit
  words. Then one merge CTA a row works from the exact counts. If the floor is the
  K-th key, it takes every gt token plus the first eq tokens. If at most 8,192 tokens
  are candidates, it runs a radix select over them. Otherwise it falls back to the
  whole-row select. Ties go to the lower position, the output is ascending, and the
  count stays 2048 past the dense prefix and 0 within it. Flash's visible/4 pool rule
  is not used.

The scan/merge scheme is the one in patch 0067's tf_glm_dsel_scan/merge (the attn5
overnight work, 2026-10-31, glm_index_decode.cuh), applied here to per-token keys. Its
select_core gains an output-type parameter (int32 here). The design follows Codex's
analysis (docs/gpt-sol-new.md section 4.4). Every existing kernel keeps its SASS
(cuobjdump, 12 checked, including tf_glm_dsa_scores, tf_glm_dsa_topk and
tf_glm_dsel_merge/scan).

cuda_native.policyBoot offers both bits on glm_moe_dsa. Their kernels sit in
glm_glue, and their scratch sits in every decode window's buffers on every rank.
Forward.set() ORs the boot bits into glue.Set.policy.

Proof (spark5 GB10, 1,787-1,794 MHz):
- dsaSelect, bits set vs clear, in one process (test-glm-swift-krn-gpu with
  production-flag fatbins): 160/160 cases are byte-identical in scores, lists and
  counts, and qi is unchanged. The cases are rows 1-16 and lengths 2047 to 245,760,
  with random and duplicate keys. A broken rope fatbin and a broken merge fatbin both
  fail.
- CUDA harness at targets 4096, 1 and 1e6 (3 x 360 checks, 0 fail): six cases
  (all-equal keys, zero query with +-0, duplicate keys, half-zero, a cluster and
  random) at lengths 2047/2048/2049/8192/8193/30K/60K/100K/200K/245,760 and rows 1-16.
  Hoisted loops 1/2/4/8 were checked against the old scores. All four merge paths ran,
  including the fallback.
- Host: test-glm-policy-host has 23 (the 19 before + 4: each bit's rule, the 0..63
  mask table and the boot switches). The other GLM host steps match the base.

Time saved for 21 full-indexer layers per round, cold key caches:
- 60K keys: 0.8 / 1.8 / 2.7-3.3 ms at 1 / 4 / 9 rows.
- 200K keys: 3.3 / 13.2 / 26.9 ms at 1 / 4 / 9 rows.
- At 4,096 and 8,192 keys there is no penalty.

